{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested for lane B revision 3 at exact head 7d9e8da0231786ef5045d6da8fefa698b640af93, resting on all 46 artifacts with notes/2026-10-01-laneB-git.md first.\n\nValidation: clean installation, repository strict type checks, 101 Node tests with real git/SQLite and 8 workerd tests passed independently. Three additional local fault-injection tests reproduce two remaining credential-cleanup defects through Workspaces' public API. Their file is /tmp/artroom-checker-git-v3/packages/git/test/checker-v3-review.test.ts. The tests use the submitted fake Artifacts namespace and real SQLite; they do not establish that the live service has exhibited these failures. Reviewed all changed artifact bytes and the new live-run JSON, retaining previous inspection of identical files. The two novel lock descriptors are development-only @types/node 24.19.0 and its undici-types 7.24.6 dependency, installed successfully. No production source was changed, and no live deploy, remote token mint or measurement driver was run.\n\nReview b78a837f: durable cleanup records and host alarm scheduling repair the demonstrated failed-inventory and failed-revocation cases; fork locks and lease-fenced failure writes repair the two demonstrated overlapping-operation races; pending renewal now records the deadline and rereads it after awaits; the ordered bounded tree-read window repairs the full-level prefetch defect. Keep these tests. The cleanup requirement remains partly open for the two unknown-outcome cases below.\n\n1. [P1, Security/cleanup after restart] An empty inventory or absent fork does not prove an outstanding remote create/mint will never apply. In packages/git/src/workspace/workspaces.ts:424-426 and 438-456, cleanFork resolves the durable inventory obligation on one absent-fork or completed inventory response. A restart loses the in-memory fork lock and can run this cleanup while the old remote request remains outstanding. The independent fork test holds canonical.fork before its remote side effect, constructs a fresh Workspaces on the same SQL, reconciles the still-absent fork, and observes zero cleanup obligations and nextDue null. The remote then creates the fork and its 24-hour write token; its answer never reaches the stopped original host. Subsequent reconciliation still reports zero with that token active. The mint variant holds createToken before remote application and reproduces the same premature completion against an existing positively verified fork. The submitted crash test covers a token already visible before restart; it does not cover remote application after cleanup. Persist the ownership and unknown outcome of each non-idempotent remote step. Do not discharge its cleanup duty merely from a snapshot taken before it can finish, and do not allow another credential installation to race an unresolved old mint. Use an actual completion fence or documented idempotent operation identity/termination guarantee; if the binding cannot prove completion, retain a scheduled recovery/inventory duty and the unresolved state. Preserve exact fork provenance and the successful restart tests. Add both delayed-remote-application tests, including a later lease and no callback from the stopped host.\n\n2. [P2, Security/retry inventory] A successful retry does not account for all tokens created by earlier attempts. At workspaces.ts:316-334, withRetry wraps createToken, and installation resolves mintDebt directly because the returned token is now recorded. An earlier attempt may have minted a token before returning a retryable INTERNAL_ERROR (10400). The independent test uses the submitted mintAfter hook to throw that error after minting; the next attempt succeeds. Provision returns ready with two active write tokens, pendingCleanup zero, nextDue null, and reconcile leaves both active. The interface provides no idempotency key or guarantee that a retryable response implies no remote side effect. Account for each attempt and settle orphan inventory while preserving only the installed token before claiming ready/one live token. Add a retry-after-application test and inventory/revocation-failure variants. If relying on stronger upstream semantics instead, make that guarantee explicit and verified; an ordinary retry wrapper is not that guarantee.\n\nArchitecture: the durable cleanup queue, current lease checks and bounded diff pipeline are sound improvements. Cleanup completion still needs to describe the remote operation's outcome rather than one observed inventory snapshot. Retain the publication rule that an unknown push holds its slot and completes forward with the same integration and expected main. The harness remains a stand-in; the required production Room integration and full acceptance matrix are not waived by passing package tests.\n\nSecurity: positive fork provenance and before-await cleanup records are useful safeguards. Complete the unknown-outcome and retry cases before asserting that a ready workspace has exactly one lease-bound write token or that restart revokes every orphan. No deployed exploit or live-service occurrence is claimed.\n\nSimplification: keep one durable protocol for fork creation, mint attempts, known-token revocation and inventory recovery, with explicit unresolved and completed states and one scheduling contract. Avoid separate ad hoc retry semantics that discard this ownership. The in-memory lock may coordinate one live host, but cannot serve as restart evidence.\n\nThe submitted live run records two successful disjoint landings, a named conflict, bounded diff refusal, mainHasBoth true and releasedTokenRefused true. It supports the ordinary harness loop, not the two failure cases above. Full scope remains the acceptance target; estimates do not reduce it.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:517d669b466f5c78103b3ff97a7004f5992bb588","binding":"assigned","head":"7d9e8da0231786ef5045d6da8fefa698b640af93","head_news